.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9852147</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib46"><citation citation-type="journal">Suomalainen, M., M. Nakano, S. Keller, K. Boucke, R.P. Stidwill, and U.F. Greber. <year>1999</year>. Microtubule-dependent plus and minus end-directed motilities are competing processes for nuclear targeting of adenovirus. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>144</volume>:<fpage>657</fpage>&#x02013;672.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10037788</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><citation citation-type="journal">Tooze, J., M. Hollinshead, B. Reis, K. Radsak, and H. Kern. <year>1993</year>. Progeny vaccinia and human cytomegalovirus particles utilize early endosomal cisternae for their envelopes. <source>Eur. J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>60</volume>:<fpage>163</fpage>&#x02013;178.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8385018</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib48"><citation citation-type="journal">Trinczek, B., A. Ebneth, E.M. Mandelkov, and E. Mandelkov. <year>1999</year>. Tau regulates the attachment/detachment but not the speed of motors in microtubule-dependent transport of single vesicles and organelles. <source>J. Cell Sci.</source> <volume>112</volume>:<fpage>2355</fpage>&#x02013;2367.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10381391</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib49"><citation citation-type="journal">van Eijl, H., M. Hollinshead, and G.L. Smith. <year>2000</year>. The vaccinia virus A36R protein is a type Ib membrane protein present on intracellular but not extracellular enveloped particles. <source>Virology</source>. <volume>271</volume>:<fpage>26</fpage>&#x02013;36.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10814567</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib50"><citation citation-type="journal">Wolffe, E.J., S.N. Isaacs, and B. Moss. <year>1993</year>. Deletion of the vaccinia virus B5R gene encoding a 42-kiloDalton membrane glycoprotein inhibits extracellular virus envelope formation and dissemination. <source>J. Virol.</source> <volume>67</volume>:<fpage>4732</fpage>&#x02013;4741.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8331727</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib51"><citation citation-type="journal">Wolffe, E.J., E. Katz, A. Weisberg, and B. Moss. <year>1997</year>. The A34R glycoprotein gene is required for induction of specialized actin-containing microvilli and efficient cell-to-cell transmission of vaccinia virus. <source>J. Virol.</source> <volume>71</volume>:<fpage>3904</fpage>&#x02013;3915.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9094667</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib52"><citation citation-type="journal">Wolffe, E.J., A.S. Weisberg, and B. Moss. <year>1998</year>. Role for the vaccinia virus A36R outer envelope protein in the formation of virus-tipped actin-containing microvilli and cell-to-cell virus spread. <source>Virology</source>. <volume>244</volume>:<fpage>20</fpage>&#x02013;26.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9581774</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib53"><citation citation-type="journal">Zhang, W.-H., D. Wilcock, and G.L. Smith. <year>2000</year>. The vaccinia virus F12L protein is required for actin tail formation, normal plaque size and virulence. <source>J. Virol.</source> <volume>74</volume>:<fpage>11663</fpage>&#x02013;11670.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11090165</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1"><label>*</label><p>Abbreviations used in this paper: CEV, cell-associated enveloped virus&#x0003b; EEV, extracellular enveloped virus&#x0003b; EGFP, enhanced green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; hpi, hours postinfection&#x0003b; IEV, intracellular enveloped virus&#x0003b; IMCBH, <italic>N</italic><sub>1</sub>-isonicotinoyl-<italic>N</italic><sub>2</sub>-3-methyl-4-chlorobenzoylhydrazine&#x0003b; IMV, intracellular mature virus&#x0003b; mAb, monoclonal antibody&#x0003b; PP1, 4-amino-5-(4-methylphenyl)-7-(t-butyl)pyrazolo(3,4-d)pyrimidine&#x0003b; VV, vaccinia virus&#x0003b; WR, Western reserve.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>